he water vapor are able to pass through, but very slowly. They aren't trying to let a huge gust of wind through; it's the slow escape of individual molecules that we are trying to convey. It's not like an open window. Now what about the polyurethane (PU) membrane types? They *are* technically solid, but unlike the plastic bag (say, polyethylene) they are *hydrophilic*. They *like* water. So they're not a barrier like a plastic bag; instead they actively *attract* and "move" water vapor through, like a molecular conveyor belt. The important thing is this "molecular level" difference. Okay, so the response is going to be: first, I validate their point. Then, I contrast the plastic bag vs. membrane idea, using the screen door analogy to explain the size/scale difference. Then explain *why* the plastic bag "fails." No pores, wrong chemistry. Ultimately, it is the structure of the materials that makes it work, not that they are impermeable barriers.
14:26:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your common sense is exactly right! If you put on a rubber glove or wear a plastic trash bag as a poncho, you will stay 100% dry from the rain, but you will quickly become soaked in your own sweat. That is exactly the problem that jacket manufacturers spent decades trying to solve. The reason a plastic bag or a rubber glove holds both water *and* air inside is because they are made of a **continuous, solid sheet of material** (like polyethylene or latex). To use an analogy, a plastic bag is like a solid concrete wall. Nothing gets in, and nothing gets out—not liquid, and not gas. Waterproof/breathable jackets are fundamentally different from plastic bags. Here is why they don't act like rubber gloves, using a helpful analogy: ### The "Chain-Link Fence" Analogy Instead of a solid concrete wall, imagine a chain-link fence. * If you throw a **giant beach ball** at the fence, it bounces off. It’s too big to fit through the holes. * If you throw a handful of **sand** at the fence, the sand passes right through the holes and to the other side. In a breathable jacket (like Gore-Tex), the membrane acts like that fence. * A **raindrop** is the giant beach ball. Even though water is a liquid, surface tension binds millions of water molecules together into a droplet. That droplet is physically too massive to fit through the microscopic holes in the jacket. * **Water vapor (sweat)** is the sand. When your body heat turns sweat into a gas, the water molecules break apart from one another. A single, floating molecule of water vapor is incredibly tiny—plenty small enough to drift right through the "chain-link fence" of the jacket and escape into the air. ### Why doesn't a plastic bag do this? Because a plastic bag doesn't have those microscopic holes. It is completely sealed. Creating a material that has holes small enough to block a raindrop, but large enough to let vapor escape, is a massive feat of engineering. When Gore-Tex was invented in 1969, it was a massive breakthrough precisely because it defied the "rubber glove" common sense. They figured out how to take a type of plastic (Teflon) and stretch it rapidly so that it tore on a microscopic level, creating billions of tiny pores that turned a "concrete wall" into a "chain-link fence." So, your intuition is completely correct for normal plastics and rubbers. Waterproof/breathable jackets only work because they are made of highly engineered, microscopic meshes that trick nature by sorting water by its size!
